--TEST--
`Core\Server::isDraining` answers the `bool` itself — it binds to one, its negation inverts it and its one conversion is the textual one, with no falsy stand-in behind any of the three — ADR 0097 § 5, ADR 0063 R4
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The question here is the *type* of the answer, not its value. ADR 0063 R4
// keeps a `Core` member from reporting a state through a falsy return, and the
// three readings below are the ones a caller writes: the binding, which a
// non-`bool` return would not compile against; the negation, which a value
// that were merely falsy would still satisfy; and the textual reading, which
// is the only conversion `rule:types/conversion` gives a `bool` — a number out of a
// predicate is a branch said out loud, so `$answer as int` does not compile
// and is deliberately not what this case asks.
bool $answer = Core\Server::isDraining();

if ($answer) {
    echo "bound: draining\n";
} else {
    echo "bound: accepting\n";
}

// The negation of the same binding, named on the same run: a member answering
// one of these and not the other would be answering two different questions.
if (!$answer) {
    echo "negated: not draining\n";
} else {
    echo "negated: draining\n";
}

// The conversion, delimited so an empty rendering is still visible on the
// line — which is the reading that would hide a `null` or an empty string
// standing in for the `bool`.
echo "as string: [", ($answer as string), "]\n";

// And the member read directly, without the binding between: the two are one
// call, so a member whose answer depended on being bound first would part here.
if (!Core\Server::isDraining()) {
    echo "direct: not draining\n";
} else {
    echo "direct: draining\n";
}
--EXPECT--
bound: accepting
negated: not draining
as string: []
direct: not draining
